 ### [TensoRF Training Configuration](18549-tensorf-training-configuration.md)
-Not given in the sources.
+This configuration defines the training protocol for the TensoRF representation in the context of sparse-view lung nodule volumetry from digitally reconstructed radiographs (DRRs). It addresses the instability of standard TensoRF density parameterization, which causes zero gradient flow during early training, and introduces anatomy-aware regularization to prevent overfitting in highly under-constrained settings (18:1 parameter-to-ray ratio). It sets itself apart from standard NeRF variants by using vector-matrix (VM) tensor factorization for the density field and specific initialization shifts to ensure non-zero density gradients.
 
 ## Background
-The concept of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 . This work established the paradigm of using MLPs to represent continuous 3D scenes for novel view synthesis. Subsequent works, such as PixelNeRF (Yu et al., 2021), extended this to sparse-view settings by incorporating CNN-extracted features . The Attenuation Field approach adapts this implicit representation for medical X-ray imaging, replacing the radiance model with the Beer–Lambert law .
